{"records":[{"id":"98nyhrvfvz","title":"Antagonistic effects of mitochondrial matrix and intermembrane space proteases on yeast aging. Vega M. et al","description":"In many organisms, aging is characterized by a loss of mitochondrial homeostasis. Multiple factors such as respiratory metabolism, mitochondrial fusion/fission, or mitophagy have been linked to cell longevity, but the exact impact of each one on the aging process is still unclear.\nUsing the deletion mutant collection of the fission yeast Schizosaccharomyces pombe, we have developed a genome-wide screening for mutants with altered chronological lifespan. We have identified four mutants associated with proteolysis at the mitochondria that exhibit opposite effects on longevity. The analysis of the respiratory activity of these mutants revealed a positive correlation between increased respiration rate and prolonged lifespan. We also found that the phenotype of the long-lived protease mutants could not be explained by impaired mitochondrial fusion/fission activities, but it was dependent on mitophagy induction. The anti-aging role of mitophagy was supported by the effect of a mutant defective in degradation of mitochondria, which shortened lifespan of the long-lived mutants.\nOur characterization of the mitochondrial protease mutants demonstrates that mitophagy sustains the lifespan extension of long-lived mutants displaying a higher respiration potential.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-20T21:05:02.625Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/98nyhrvfvz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/98nyhrvfvz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Montserrat","last_name":"Vega","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Aging","Yeast"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jh7h5cx4yh","title":"TNIP1 inhibits selective autophagy via bipartite interaction with LC3/GABARAP and TAX1BP1","description":"Mitophagy is a form of selective autophagy that disposes of superfluous and potentially damage-inducing organelles in a tightly controlled manner. While the machinery involved in mitophagy induction is well known, the regulation of the components is less clear. Here, we demonstrate that TNIP1 knock out accelerates mitophagy rates, and that ectopic TNIP1 negatively regulates the rate of mitophagy. These functions of TNIP1 depend on an evolutionarily conserved LIR motif as well as an AHD3 domain, which are required for binding to the LC3/GABARAP family of proteins and the autophagy receptor TAX1BP1, respectively. We further show that phosphorylation appears to regulate its association with the ULK1 complex member FIP200, allowing TNIP1 to compete with autophagy receptors, providing a molecular rationale for its inhibitory function during mitophagy. Taken together, our findings identify a novel negative regulator of mitophagy that acts at the early steps of autophagosome biogenesis. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-02-08T11:02:24.139Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jh7h5cx4yh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jh7h5cx4yh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"François","last_name":"Le Guerroué","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Autophagy","Mitophagy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rmz22dfn8j","title":"ATG4 family proteins drive phagophore growth independently of the LC3/GABARAP lipidation system_Nguyen et al","description":"The sequestration of damaged mitochondria within double-membrane structures termed autophagosomes is a key step of PINK1/Parkin mitophagy. The ATG4 family of proteases are thought to regulate autophagosome formation exclusively by processing the ubiquitin-like ATG8 family (LC3/GABARAPs). We discover that human ATG4s promote autophagosome formation independently of their protease activity and of ATG8 family processing. ATG4 proximity networks reveal a role for ATG4s and their proximity partners, including the immune-disease protein LRBA, in ATG9A vesicle trafficking to mitochondria. Artificial intelligence-directed 3D electron microscopy of phagophores shows that ATG4s promote phagophore-ER contacts during the lipid-transfer phase of autophagosome formation. We also show that ATG8 removal during autophagosome maturation does not depend on ATG4 activity. Instead, we find that ATG4s can disassemble ATG8-protein conjugates, revealing a role for ATG4s as deubiquitinating-like enzymes. These findings establish non-canonical roles of the ATG4 family beyond the ATG8 lipidation axis and provide an AI-driven framework for rapid 3D electron microscopy.\n","source":{"id":"7628d440-43d3-4f11-9cb4-7b195c8180d5","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-26T22:16:15.307Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rmz22dfn8j","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rmz22dfn8j"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Thanh Ngọc","last_name":"Nguyen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Benjamin Scott ","last_name":"Padman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Susanne ","last_name":"Zellner","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Grace ","last_name":"Khuu","orcid":null},{"first_name":" Louise ","last_name":"Uoselis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wai Kit ","last_name":"Lam","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marvin ","last_name":"Skulsuppaisarn","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Runa S. J. ","last_name":"Lindblom","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Emily M. ","last_name":"Watts","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian ","last_name":"Behrends","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael ","last_name":"Lazarou","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Confocal Microscopy","Electron Microscopy","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s3jtdhjdbs","title":"Supplementary data for Michaelis et al., 'Protein import motor complex reacts to mitochondrial misfolding by reducing protein import and activating mitophagy'","description":"Supplementary data for Michaelis et al., 'Mitochondrial import loss induces mitophagy without membrane depolarization'. We hypothesized that inhibition of mitochondrial protein import induced mitophagy independent of mitochondrial depolarization. This dataset contains raw Illumina sequencing data for all samples from a genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 screen for mitophagy regulators. The genome-wide library is Brunello from Doench et al Nat Biotechnol. 2016. We found that the pre-sequence translocase-associated motor complex PAM controls mitophagy induction. Loss of PAM caused a loss in protein import and was sufficient to induce mitophagy without depolarization.\nThe samples have been sequenced on an Illumina NextSeq 500 in single end mode with 75+8 cycles. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-08-05T15:38:53.752Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s3jtdhjdbs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s3jtdhjdbs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jonas","last_name":"Michaelis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Melinda","last_name":"Brunstein","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Süleyman","last_name":"Bozkurt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ludovico","last_name":"Alves","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Wegner","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Manuel","last_name":"Kaulich","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Pohl","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Münch","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics","Mitochondrion","Mitophagy","Genetic Screening","Next Generation Sequencing","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z46cyfd8tz","title":"Parkin_mediated_mitophagy_is_induced_by_the_probiotics_Saccharomyces_boulardii_and_Lactococcus_lactis","description":"Dysfunctional mitochondria are a hallmark feature of neurodegenerative disorders, such as Parkinson’s\ndisease, and upregulation of Parkin-mediated mitophagy pathways serve to remove unhealthy\nmitochondria from cells. Probiotics have been used to alleviate symptoms of Parkinson’s disease and\nthere is evidence to suggest that they modulate mitochondrial pathways. In this study, we conducted a\nscreen of 49 probiotics and tested various stages of mitophagy to determine whether any of the\nprobiotics can upregulate mitophagy in cells sustaining mitochondrial damage. We found that two\nprobiotics, Saccharomyces boulardii and Lactococcus lactis upregulate mitochondrial Parkin recruitment,\nphospho-ubiquitination, and mitofusin degradation. Further, these strains increase lysosomal\ndegradation of dysfunctional mitochondria in the brain of Drosophila that were subjected to the\nmitochondrial toxin paraquat, and also improve longevity and motor function in this Drosophila model of\nParkinson’s disease. These effects occur due to exogenous soluble factors that are released by probiotics\nunder laboratory conditions. We identified methyl-isoquinoline-6-carboxylate as one such molecule,\nwhich upregulates mitochondrial parkin recruitment, phospho-ubiquitination, mitofusin degradation, and\nlysosomal degradation, while restoring motor function in paraquat-treated flies. These data suggest a\nnovel mechanism that probiotics use to stimulate mitophagy through a Parkin-dependent pathway that\ncould potentially be utilized to treat neurological diseases at an early stage.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-09-29T09:55:33.564Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z46cyfd8tz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z46cyfd8tz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Peter","last_name":"Hawrysh","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mitophagy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tjx2nbm2x8","title":"Mitochondrial fumarate inhibits Parkin-mediated mitophagy. Su Jin Ham et al.","description":"Here, we explore the potential involvement of fumarate, a metabolite generated from the citric acid cycle, as a key regulator of PINK1-Parkin-mediated mitophagy. Fumarate engages in a process called succination, forming S-(2-succino) cysteine through a Michael-like reaction with protein cysteine residues in the cell. Our research demonstrates that this modification specifically targets the sulfhydryl group of cysteine 323 and 451 residues of human Parkin, leading to the inhibition of its mitochondrial localization and E3 ligase activity, thereby impeding PINK1-Parkin-mediated mitophagy. Notably, our investigation reveals that the succinatable cysteines in human Parkin are not conserved in invertebrates, including Drosophila. To assess the functional impact of Parkin succination, we generated Parkin knock-in flies with succinatable cysteines. These flies exhibited robust Parkinson's disease (PD)-related phenotypes when exposed to elevated fumarate levels. Collectively, our findings underscore the significance of fumarate as an endogenous regulator of PINK1-Parkin-mediated mitophagy, offering insights into the intricate interplay between mitochondrial metabolic activities and PD pathology. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-17T19:27:20.498Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tjx2nbm2x8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tjx2nbm2x8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jongkyeong","last_name":"Chung","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"RS-2024-00440824"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00y0zf565","name":"Institute for Basic Science","grant_id":"IBS-R008-D1"},{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"NRF-2022R1I1A1A0105543312"},{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"NRF-2021R1A2C1010577"},{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"RS-2023-00211029"},{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"NRF-2020R1A5A1018081 "}],"categories":["Parkinson's Disease","Post-Translational Modification","Mitophagy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vhtn5s5dbx","title":"Ca2+/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase II β (CaMKIIβ) decodes ER Ca2+ transients to trigger autophagosome formation. Qiaoxia Zheng et al.","description":"These are unprocessed and uncompressed imaging data (microscopy as well as gels and blots) of the study \"Ca2+/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase II β (CaMKIIβ) decodes ER Ca2+ transients to trigger autophagosome formation\". In this study, we focused on how Ca2+ transients on the ER outer surface elicited by autophagy stimuli are sustained and decoded to trigger autophagosome formation in multicellular organisms. Here we show that CaMKIIβ integrates ER Ca2+ transients to trigger liquid-liquid phase separation (LLPS) of the autophagosome-initiating FIP200 complex. In response to ER Ca2+ transients, CaMKIIβ is recruited from actin filaments and forms condensates, which serve as sites for emergence of or interaction with FIP200 puncta. CaMKIIβ phosphorylates FIP200 at Thr269, Thr1127 and Ser1484 to modulate LLPS and properties of the FIP200 complex, thereby controlling its function in autophagosome formation. CaMKIIβ also controls the amplitude, duration and propagation of ER Ca2+ transients during autophagy induction. CaMKIIβ mutations identified in the neurodevelopmental disorder MRD54 affect the function of CaMKIIβ in autophagy. Our study reveals that CaMKIIβ is essential for sustaining and decoding ER Ca2+ transients to specify autophagosome formation in metazoans.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-12-20T12:14:55.172Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vhtn5s5dbx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vhtn5s5dbx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hong","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qiaoxia","last_name":"Zheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Huan","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"32322038"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82188101"}],"categories":["Western Blot","Super-Resolution Imaging","Cellular Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"d4jbd66xdd","title":"A mitophagy sensor PPTC7 integrates homeostatic and physiological signals to regulate mitophagy and mitochondrial mass - Sun et al","description":" Unprocessed and uncompressed imaging data (microscopy as well as gels and blots) from A mitophagy sensor PPTC7 integrates homeostatic and physiological signals to regulate mitophagy and mitochondrial mass - Sun et al. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-31T20:08:58.821Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/d4jbd66xdd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/d4jbd66xdd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yuqiu","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Immunofluorescence"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"83vvm47z2f","title":"TEMs for the qualitative assessment of mitophagy in LHON cells","description":"Qualitative TEM mitophagy activity assessment on LHON 3460 fibroblasts (mito=mitochondria; AV=autophagy vacuoles; Arrow= autophagy vacuole membrane) (A). TEMs for the qualitative assessment of RGCs in human post-mortem retinas are represented in the following panels: (B) On the left, an axon hillock (bordered with red lines, left panel) of an RGC\nfrom an LHON 3460-affected patient can be observed with numerous mitochondria (M) at the entrance of its axon with its characteristic cytoskeletal elements. Mitochondria have been distinguished accordingly to the presence of mitochondrial cristae. On the right, an RGC from an LHON 11778 affected patient with a mitochondrion (inset, right panel) is surrounded by an isolation membrane (IM) in the process of forming an autophagosome vesicle documenting an early stage of mitophagy (right panel). (C) Post-mortem TEM images of RGCs of LHON-carrier 11778 characterized by prominent cytoplasmic vacuolization due to a high-level of compensatory mitobiogenesis. In the lower left panel, a red line marks the limits of the two RGCs present in the TEM image shown. On the right, higher magnification with relative inset of an RGC from an LHON 11778-carrier patient where it is possible to well distinguish the increased mitochondrial biogenesis of an LHON-carrier individual. Mitochondria (M) have been distinguished accordingly to the presence of mitochondrial cristae.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-20T17:47:08.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/83vvm47z2f","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/83vvm47z2f"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alberto","last_name":"Danese","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Transmission Electron Microscopy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"t5m4stxyfm","title":"Urolithin A prevents age-related hearing loss in C57BL/6J mice likely by mitophagy induction ","description":"Figure 1. Effect of Urolithin A (UA) on hearing and the mitochondrial state.\nFigure 2. Effect of Urolithin A (UA) on expression levels of mitophagy-associated molecules and OXPHOS proteins in the cochlea.\nFigure 3. Effect of Urolithin A (UA) on expression levels of mitophagolysosomes and major mitophagy players in the cochlea.\nFigure 4. Effect of Urolithin A (UA) on expression levels of mitophagy-associated molecules and OXPHOS proteins in the auditory cortex. \nFigure 5. Effect of Urolithin A (UA) on expression levels of mitophagolysosomes and major mitophagy players in the central auditory system. \n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-30T20:48:30.429Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t5m4stxyfm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t5m4stxyfm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sung Il","last_name":"Cho","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eu-Ri","last_name":"Jo","orcid":"0000-0002-8985-8091"},{"first_name":"Hee Sun","last_name":"Jang","orcid":"0009-0009-7814-1114"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"2022R1A2C1005091"}],"categories":["Aging","Mitophagy","Hearing Loss"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":139608,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":439,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":139608,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":138976,"restricted":67,"embargoed":565},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2336},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1484},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":677},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":477},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":451},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":436},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":420},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":382},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":308},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":274}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":136954},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1909},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":186},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":138},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":77},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":43},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":1019},{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":954},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":522},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":368}]}}